Syntax colouring rules
======================

One file per file type, plus common.jvsyn and filetype.jvsyn. They are ordinary
ex commands, so ":source" is all that reads them.

    an rc                    source $VIM/syntax/filetype.jvsyn
      filetype.jvsyn         source $VIM/syntax/common.jvsyn     always
        "begin suffixes=.go  source $VIM/syntax/go.jvsyn         for a .go file
        "end   suffixes

$VIM is the directory these live in. On Windows the editor sets it to the one
holding the exe, which is where the package unpacks to, so it is already right.
On a Unix it is the directory the build was installed to -- /usr/local/lib/jvim3
unless PREFIX said otherwise -- and running the editor out of a build tree needs
VIM set by hand.

common.jvsyn first
------------------

It clears whatever the last buffer left behind, defines the ten user colours and
gives every group name -- Comment, String, Statement and the rest -- a colour.
The per-type files use those names and nothing else, so a rule set is one file
and re-colouring the whole editor is one line in common.jvsyn. A file sourced
without common.jvsyn having run first reports "Invalid argument" for every rule
in it, because the names mean nothing yet.

Every group name, down to one that a single rule file uses: Arg and Attr, which
are rc.jvsyn's; HtmlTag, HtmlExt, HtmlArg and HtmlEmbed; DiffAdd and DiffDel;
MdHead and MdCode; Url, E-Mail, Value. Defining one in the file that uses it
works for every file but a .jvsyn, and a .jvsyn is opened with common.jvsyn and
jvimrc.jvsyn and nothing else -- so DiffAdd was not a colour while diff.jvsyn
was the file on screen, and the line that gave DiffAdd its colour came out
plain. A new group name is two lines: the "syntax link" here, and the
"syntax <name> w/<name>" in jvimrc.jvsyn that draws the name in its own colour.

Adding a file type
------------------

Write syntax/zig.jvsyn:

    syntax Comment			n/\/\/.*$
    syntax String			n/\"\([^\"\\]\\|\\.\)*\"
    syntax Statement		w/fn/return/defer/comptime

and add the block to filetype.jvsyn that reaches it:

    "begin suffixes=.zig
    "source $VIM/syntax/zig.jvsyn
    "end   suffixes

The leading " is not a typo: the block is a comment until the name of the file
being opened matches, and 'fexrc' is what takes the quote off. Match by name
rather than by suffix with "begin files=Makefile;GNUmakefile.

These files colour themselves. A .jvsyn is the same language as the "syntax"
lines of an rc, so it reads jvimrc.jvsyn, which draws every group name in its
own group and every colour name in its own colour -- Error is red there because
Error is red, and the colour list is a legible palette rather than a list of
words. Only a quote on a line of its own starts a comment in one, because a
pattern is full of \" and any-quote-starts-a-comment would paint the pattern of
every String rule as a comment.

Checking what a rule did
------------------------

A rule that matches the wrong thing says so in no other way: the screen comes
out a colour short, and which of two hundred rules is responsible is anyone's
guess. So ask it.

    :syntax dump /tmp/out

writes one line per coloured run of the buffer:

    3:4-6 Conditional w/if
    3:11-15 Number w/0x\x\+

The line, the byte offsets (the second one past the end), the group, and the
rule as the editor received it. Text no rule claimed is not listed, so an empty
dump means nothing matched at all. It walks the buffer through the same code the
screen draws from, so what it says is what would be drawn, and it needs no GUI:

    jvim3 -s cmds file.py

with cmds holding  :syntax dump /tmp/out<CR>:q!<CR>  -- one command, no
screenshots, and the answer is text.

The pattern comes back the way the editor got it, after the ex command line has
eaten a backslash: a rule written  m/\".*\"  prints as  m/".*"  . That is the
level the regexp actually sees, and a quick way to check an escape.

A rule with two or three patterns is named by the one that matched: a region (p)
by the pattern that opens it, a tag rule (t) by the third, what it looks for
inside the tag. Naming a tag rule by its first would print  t/<  for every rule
in html.jvsyn.

../scripts/test-syntax.sh is that loop written down -- a few lines of a language
and the dump they should produce. Change a rule, run it, read the diff. Add a
case for anything you fix: three lines, and it is the only thing that will
notice when the rule stops working.

Writing rules
-------------

../doc.j/readme.doc section 6.26 is the reference. Seven things it does not say:

  o The leftmost match on the line wins, and where two rules match at the same
    column the one earlier in the file does. Put comments and strings at the
    top, and a narrower rule before the wider one it would otherwise lose to.

  o ":syntax" is an ex command, so '"' starts a trailing comment and '|' ends
    the command before the rule is ever looked at. The ex line eats one
    backslash from each of them, which is the whole of the rule:

        \"      one backslash, and the regexp gets a plain "
        |       no backslash: the command stops there, and the rest of the
                line is run as another command -- n/aaa|ccc quietly becomes
                the rule n/aaa and a command "ccc"
        \|      one backslash, and the regexp gets a plain | to match
        \\|     two, so the regexp gets \| -- which is an alternation

    So a rule can hold an alternation, written with two backslashes:

        syntax PreCondit  n/^\s*#\s*\(ifdef\\|ifndef\\|if\\|else\)

    The branches are tried left to right at each position, so a longer one
    goes before a prefix of itself: ifdef before if, or #ifdef comes out as
    #if with "def" left plain.

    Not in a w rule. That one wraps the whole pattern in \< \>, so w/a\\|b
    compiles as \<a\|b\> -- "\<a" or "b\>" -- and colours the a in "ab". A w
    rule already takes a list, separated by the delimiter: w/a/b.

    A list is also much the faster of the two, which is not the way round it
    looks. Each word of a w list becomes its own rule, but a word rule is
    looked up in an index of the line built once per line (syn_strstr() in
    ../src/syntax.c), so a keyword that is not there costs nothing to rule out.
    An alternation is a regexp and has to be run at each position instead.
    Rewriting every w list in c.jvsyn as one alternation -- 61 words, identical
    colouring -- made a 600-redraw scroll of src/syntax.c 2.4 times slower.
    Keep word lists as w lists; use an alternation for what w cannot say.

    That index is built one character class at a time, so a w rule can only
    ever match a run of one class. "E-Mail" is three runs -- "E", "-", "Mail"
    -- and w/E-Mail is a rule that never matches anything, silently. Write it
    out as n/\<E-Mail\>, which is the \< \> a w rule would have wrapped it in.

  o The regexp is vim's "magic": . * [ ^ $ bare, and \+ \= \< \> \( \) with a
    backslash, plus the classes \i \k \f \p \s \d \x \o \w \h \a \l \u and
    their upper case complements. There is no \{n,m} and nothing non-greedy.

  o A string is written  n/\"\([^\"\\]\\|\\.\)*\"  -- a quote, then any number
    of (one character that is neither a quote nor a backslash, or a backslash
    and whatever follows it), then a quote. Copy it; do not simplify it.

    Every rule file said  m/\".*[^\\]\"  until 2026-09-01: a quote, anything,
    one character that is not a backslash, a quote. That reads as "ends at a
    quote that is not escaped" and is not: an empty "" has no character between
    its quotes and "C:\\" has a backslash there, so neither could end where it
    does, and the match ran on to the next quote on the line with everything in
    between drawn as string. The line  textmode ? "" : "[notextmode] "  in this
    tree's own fileio.c came out as one run from the first quote to the last. The form above cannot
    reach past a closing quote, because neither branch of the alternation matches
    one. It is also faster -- 8 times, on a 33 kB minified .js on one line --
    since it gives up at the first character that cannot be in a string instead
    of scanning to the end of the line from every quote.

    Single quoted: the same with ' for ". A language whose strings have no
    escapes at all (sh, sql, make) can use  n/'[^']*'  instead.

  o A colour can say what goes behind it, with "on" and a second colour:

        syntax link Error   bolic white on maroon
        syntax link DiffAdd       green on #e6ffe6
        syntax link MdCode              on #f0f0f0

    Either a name a foreground would take, or the colour itself as #rrggbb --
    which is what the rule files use, none of the sixteen named ones being pale
    enough to read text off. With nothing before the "on" the text keeps the
    colour it would have had. "text" and "reverse" are refused: neither names a
    colour, so neither can be behind anything.

    bold, italic and uline belong to the text and go before it as they always
    did; there is no such thing as a bold background.

  o The mode letters before the "/" are i (ignore case), j (fuzzy Japanese),
    w (whole words), m (shortest match), p (a region, two patterns), t (inside
    a tag), - and + (shrink or grow the match by a character), and n, which
    means nothing at all and is what the rules that want no mode are written
    with. Any other letter is refused, with a message naming it. The 2002
    manual says the rest are ignored, and they were: a rule with a typo in its
    mode went in, matched something other than what was meant, and the only
    sign of it was a colour in the wrong place.

  o p and t reach past the end of a line: a region opened on one line runs on
    into the next, and a tag rule sees into a tag whose "<" and ">" are lines
    apart. Both are remembered per line rather than searched for, so how far
    apart they are does not matter, and 'synlines' -- which is what the window
    of that search was called -- no longer does anything. Every other mode
    matches within one line.

  o One rule set cannot reach inside another. What is between <script> and
    </script> is JavaScript, and html.jvsyn has no way to hand it to
    javascript.jvsyn, so it does the one thing a single rule can: a region,
    HtmlEmbed, that puts the block on a grey ground and leaves the text the
    colour it would have had. markdown.jvsyn does the same with a fenced block.
    The opening tag is inside the region -- a region is coloured from where its
    first pattern starts, and there is nothing that says "begin after it".
